    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "DCT = DFT of a Mirror-Extended Signal",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-3081\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><text x=\"14\" y=\"12\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">one block of the frame, repeated by the DFT</text><line x1=\"14\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"232\" y2=\"64\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"1 3\"/><path d=\"M14.0,58.0 L17.6,56.2 L21.2,54.4 L24.8,52.6 L28.4,50.8 L32.0,49.0 L35.6,47.2 L39.2,45.4 L42.8,43.6 L46.4,41.8 L50.0,40.0 L53.6,38.2 L57.2,36.4 L60.8,34.6 L64.4,32.8 L68.0,31.0 L71.6,29.2 L75.2,27.4 L78.8,25.6 L82.4,23.8 L86.0,22.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><path d=\"M86.0,58.0 L89.6,56.2 L93.2,54.4 L96.8,52.6 L100.4,50.8 L104.0,49.0 L107.6,47.2 L111.2,45.4 L114.8,43.6 L118.4,41.8 L122.0,40.0 L125.6,38.2 L129.2,36.4 L132.8,34.6 L136.4,32.8 L140.0,31.0 L143.6,29.2 L147.2,27.4 L150.8,25.6 L154.4,23.8 L158.0,22.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><path d=\"M158.0,58.0 L161.6,56.2 L165.2,54.4 L168.8,52.6 L172.4,50.8 L176.0,49.0 L179.6,47.2 L183.2,45.4 L186.8,43.6 L190.4,41.8 L194.0,40.0 L197.6,38.2 L201.2,36.4 L204.8,34.6 L208.4,32.8 L212.0,31.0 L215.6,29.2 L219.2,27.4 L222.8,25.6 L226.4,23.8 L230.0,22.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><line x1=\"86\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"86\" y2=\"58\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"1 2\"/><text x=\"88\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">jump</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">mirrored for the DCT</text><line x1=\"14\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"232\" y2=\"126\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"1 3\"/><path d=\"M14.0,120.0 L17.6,118.2 L21.2,116.4 L24.8,114.6 L28.4,112.8 L32.0,111.0 L35.6,109.2 L39.2,107.4 L42.8,105.6 L46.4,103.8 L50.0,102.0 L53.6,100.2 L57.2,98.4 L60.8,96.6 L64.4,94.8 L68.0,93.0 L71.6,91.2 L75.2,89.4 L78.8,87.6 L82.4,85.8 L86.0,84.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><path d=\"M86.0,84.0 L89.6,85.8 L93.2,87.6 L96.8,89.4 L100.4,91.2 L104.0,93.0 L107.6,94.8 L111.2,96.6 L114.8,98.4 L118.4,100.2 L122.0,102.0 L125.6,103.8 L129.2,105.6 L132.8,107.4 L136.4,109.2 L140.0,111.0 L143.6,112.8 L147.2,114.6 L150.8,116.4 L154.4,118.2 L158.0,120.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><path d=\"M158.0,120.0 L161.6,118.2 L165.2,116.4 L168.8,114.6 L172.4,112.8 L176.0,111.0 L179.6,109.2 L183.2,107.4 L186.8,105.6 L190.4,103.8 L194.0,102.0 L197.6,100.2 L201.2,98.4 L204.8,96.6 L208.4,94.8 L212.0,93.0 L215.6,91.2 L219.2,89.4 L222.8,87.6 L226.4,85.8 L230.0,84.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"142\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">no jump: only cosines remain</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "A block of the street frame, repeated by the DFT, jumps where its right end meets the left (dashed). Mirrored for the DCT it is smooth, so only cosines are needed.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>What:</strong> The DCT-II treats the block as if it were <strong>mirrored</strong> at its ends, then takes a DFT of that even-symmetric extension, so only cosines remain.",
      "<strong>Why it matters:</strong> A plain DFT assumes the block repeats, creating a <strong>boundary jump</strong> that leaks energy across all frequencies; mirroring removes the jump.",
      "<strong>Result:</strong> <strong>real</strong> coefficients with far better energy compaction at block edges, which is why JPEG uses it."
    ],
    "math": "$$C(u)=\\alpha(u)\\sum_{x=0}^{N-1}f(x)\\cos\\!\\left[\\frac{(2x+1)u\\pi}{2N}\\right],\\quad \\alpha(0)=\\sqrt{\\tfrac1N},\\ \\alpha(u>0)=\\sqrt{\\tfrac2N}$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>One block of the street frame.</strong> The recorder cuts the frame into small blocks. The DFT would treat each block as repeating, creating a jump where the right end meets the left; mirroring the block removes that jump.",
    "examTrap": "The DCT is NOT the real part of the DFT. It is a different (half-sample-shifted, even-extension) transform with the factor (2x+1)/2N.",
    "stmtKind": "DEFINITION",
    "statement": "<b>Discrete cosine transform (DCT-II).</b> For a block $f(0),\\dots,f(N-1)$: $C(u)=\\alpha(u)\\sum_{x=0}^{N-1}f(x)\\cos\\!\\Big[\\frac{(2x+1)u\\pi}{2N}\\Big]$, with $\\alpha(0)=\\sqrt{1/N}$ and $\\alpha(u)=\\sqrt{2/N}$ for $u\\gt0$. It equals the DFT (up to a phase and scale) of the even, mirror-image extension of $f$, so its coefficients are real and there is no boundary discontinuity.",
    "hint": "Why does JPEG cut the frame into blocks and use cosines, not the plain DFT?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\cos\\big[(2x+1)u\\pi/2N\\big]$ = a cosine that fits the block.</strong> The $(2x+1)/2$ puts the sample in the middle of its cell, so the wave meets the block ends without a jump.",
      "<strong>$\\sum f(x)\\cos[\\cdot]$ = how much of that cosine the block has.</strong> A real number for each frequency $u$; $u=0$ is the block average.",
      "<strong>$\\alpha(u)$ = scale factors that make the transform orthonormal.</strong> Without them the basis would have unequal lengths and energy would not be preserved."
    ],
