    "title": "Worked Benchmark: Inverse vs Wiener Gain",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-3106\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><text x=\"14\" y=\"12\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">K = S\u03b7/Sf = 0.05</text><line x1=\"14\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"232\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">weak frequency, H = 0.1</text><rect x=\"26\" y=\"50\" width=\"22\" height=\"46\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><text x=\"37\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">inverse</text><text x=\"37\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">10</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"88\" width=\"22\" height=\"8\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><text x=\"71\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">Wiener</text><text x=\"71\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">1.67</text><text x=\"176\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">strong frequency, H = 1</text><rect x=\"142\" y=\"91.4\" width=\"22\" height=\"4.6\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><text x=\"153\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">inverse</text><text x=\"153\" y=\"87.4\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">1</text><rect x=\"176\" y=\"91.6\" width=\"22\" height=\"4.4\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><text x=\"187\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">Wiener</text><text x=\"187\" y=\"87.6\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">0.95</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">W(0.1) = 0.1/(0.01 + 0.05) = 1.67</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"133\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">W(1) = 1/(1 + 0.05) = 0.952</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"144\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">trade a little bias for far less noise</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "With K = 0.05: at weak H = 0.1 the inverse gain is 10 but Wiener only 1.67; at strong H = 1 they are almost equal (1 and 0.95).",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "Take $K=S_\\eta/S_f=0.05$. At a <strong>weak</strong> frequency $H=0.1$: inverse gain $1/H=\\mathbf{10}$ (noise power amplified $100\\times$), Wiener gain $=0.1/(0.01+0.05)=\\mathbf{1.67}$.",
      "At a <strong>strong</strong> frequency $H=1$: inverse gain $=1$, Wiener gain $=1/(1+0.05)=\\mathbf{0.95}$. So <strong>Wiener \u2248 inverse where the signal is strong</strong>.",
      "The 6\u00d7 lower gain at the weak frequency is the whole point: <strong>it trades a little bias for a huge cut in noise variance</strong>."
    ],
    "math": "$$W(0.1)=\\frac{0.1}{0.1^2+0.05}=1.67,\\qquad W(1)=\\frac{1}{1^2+0.05}=0.952$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Two plate strokes.</strong> A coarse stroke the camera recorded well and a fine stroke it nearly lost. The Wiener filter treats the first like the inverse does and refuses to shout at the second.",
    "examTrap": "H must be real here (no phase). With complex H use H* in the numerator and |H|\u00b2 in the denominator, not H\u00b2 or 1/H.",
    "stmtKind": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
    "statement": "<b>Example.</b> Take $K=S_\\eta/S_f=0.05$ and real $H$. At a weak frequency $H=0.1$ the inverse filter has gain $1/H=10$ (noise power $\\times100$) while the Wiener gain is $W=\\dfrac{0.1}{0.1^2+0.05}=1.67$. At a strong frequency $H=1$ the inverse gain is $1$ and $W=\\dfrac1{1+0.05}\\approx0.952$.",
    "hint": "How much quieter does the Wiener filter make the noise at a frequency where the blur left almost nothing?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$W(0.1)=0.1/(0.01+0.05)=1.67$ vs inverse $10$.</strong> At the weak frequency the Wiener gain is six times smaller, so the amplified noise power is about 36 times smaller than with the inverse.",
      "<strong>$W(1)=1/(1+0.05)=0.952$ vs inverse $1$.</strong> Where the signal is strong the two filters are almost the same: Wiener is about the inverse there.",
      "<strong>The 6\u00d7 lower gain is the point.</strong> It gives up a little accuracy (bias) to cut a huge amount of noise (variance); for complex $H$ use $H^*$ on top and $|H|^2$ below, not $1/H$."
    ],
